## Chapter 29: The Caddy’s Pass
The high-rise glass headquarters of **Vanguard Security Guild** towered thirty stories above the smog of Sector 2, its steel-and-chrome facade gleaming in the pale afternoon sun.
Inside the twenty-fourth-floor administrative office, the air was cool, smelling of ozone, expensive leather, and air filtration systems.
Senior Guild Evaluator Vane sat behind a heavy glass desk, holding a glowing digital tablet. He didn't look like an instructor or a mechanic. He wore a tailored, slate-grey tactical suit, his eyes sharp, cold, and calculating as he reviewed the four files floating on his screen.
Renna sat straight in a plush leather chair across from him, her dented tower shield resting beside her knee. Tobias and Mina sat nervously on her left, while Kaelen sat on her right, slouching slightly, his hands tucked into his worn jacket pockets.
"Let's be completely transparent, Miss Vance," Evaluator Vane said smoothly, setting the tablet onto the glass table. "Vanguard Security doesn't invest in auxiliary charity. We sponsored your squad's Class-A Academy Reserve status because your individual performance with that Tier-C Aegis Shield in Sector 4 and Sub-Level 4 demonstrates high-level vanguard potential."
Vane tapped the tablet, highlighting three secondary names.
"However, your demand that we take your entire team creates a logistical inefficiency," Vane continued, his tone devoid of malice, carrying only the flat reality of corporate metrics. "Tobias and Mina possess standard Tier-D attributes. And Kaelen Vance..." Vane turned his cold gaze onto Kaelen, scanning his frayed uniform and slouched posture. "...is a Tier-E Salvager with zero registered offensive skills."
Renna's jaw tightened, her amber eyes locking onto the evaluator. "We function as a unit, Evaluator Vane. If you want my shield on your Guild roster upon my graduation, my team stays."
Vane let out a quiet, amused breath. "And that is why we reached a compromise under **Protocol 6**."
He slid a digital stylus across the glass table toward Renna.
"**Renna Vance:** Primary Tier-A Academy Sponsorship. Full monthly stipend, access to private Guild training dungeons, and high-tier equipment allowances."
"**Tobias and Mina:** Secondary Support Reserves. 6-Month Conditional Contract. They will be granted access to the Guild's lower training facilities, subject to a mandatory performance audit every ninety days."
Vane then flicked his finger toward Kaelen.
"**Kaelen Vance:** Logged officially as **Personal Utility Caddy & Field Salvager** attached to Renna Vance's unit."
Tobias blinked, looking over at Kaelen. "A caddy?"
"In official Guild dungeons, high-tier vanguards cannot waste time harvesting monster carcasses, setting basic perimeter wires, or hauling heavy scrap packs," Vane explained, his voice indifferent. "As Renna’s utility caddy, Kaelen is granted a **Class-B Sub-Dungeon Entry Pass**. He will accompany your unit into private Guild-controlled breach zones, carry the team's salvage crates, and maintain your gear."
Vane looked directly at Kaelen, offering a dry, condescending smile.
"You get a monthly stipend of 500 credits, full access to Guild repair stations, and safety behind an Aegis Shield. All you have to do is carry the bags and clean up the blood after the real fighters clear the room. Do you accept, Mr. Vance?"
Kaelen lowered his head, putting on an embarrassed, deeply relieved expression as if he had just been handed a lifeline.
"I... I accept, sir!" Kaelen stammered meekly, his voice trembling convincingly. "I'm really good at carrying heavy packs, I promise!"
Renna placed a protective hand on Kaelen's arm, looking at Vane firmly. "He's part of our squad's survival. He stays with us."
"Then sign," Vane said flatly.
Renna took the stylus and pressed her thumb against the digital seal.
**[Vanguard Security Guild — Class-A Academy Sponsorship Finalized]**
**[Squad ID: Squad 4 (Anvil Ridge Auxiliary Division)]**
**[Personnel Logged:]**
 * **Renna Vance** — *Primary Vanguard Candidate*
 * **Tobias / Mina** — *Conditional Support Reserves*
 * **Kaelen Vance** — *Personal Utility Caddy & Field Salvager (Class-B Clearance Granted)*
Ten minutes later, the four of them stepped out of the high-rise complex onto the elevated pedestrian walkway overlooking the city.
Tobias let out a long, ragged breath, clutching his new Vanguard Guild badge. "Man... six months to pass our first audit. That evaluator didn't mince words."
"Don't worry about Vane," Renna said, turning to her team with a fierce, determined look. "We have access to the Guild's **Lower Training Vaults** now. We have three weeks before the next semester starts. We grind every single day, upgrade our gear, and prove we belong here."
She turned to Kaelen, her eyes soft with concern. "Kaelen, I know being logged as a 'caddy' sounds insulting, but—"
"I don't mind at all, Renna," Kaelen interrupted softly, offering a timid, genuine-looking smile. "It means I get to stay with the team and keep making scrap credits. That's all I need."
"You're not just a caddy to us," Renna said firmly. "Let's head back to Sector 9 and get our new schedule from Horgan."
As Renna, Tobias, and Mina walked ahead toward the transit station, chatting excitedly about their new training allowances, Kaelen fell three steps behind them.
He reached into his jacket pocket, his fingers lightly brushing the newly issued **Class-B Sub-Dungeon Entry Pass**—a sleek black metallic keycard encoded with Vanguard Guild clearance.
Under his loose uniform sleeves, his custom **Kinetic-Void Bracers** hummed with cold, suppressed power.
*A Class-B Dungeon Pass,* Kaelen thought, his dark eyes fixed on the keycard as a faint, ruthless glint flickered deep within his gaze.
To Vanguard Security, they had just hired a broke, desperate Tier-E teenager to carry bags and haul bloody monster carcasses out of private dungeons for 500 credits a month.
To Kaelen, the corporate elites had just handed him a key to private, unmonitored hunting grounds filled with Tier-B and Tier-A monsters—complete with zero municipal drones, zero public surveillance, and a legal excuse to stand over every dying beast with his bare palms open.
The caddy was ready to go to work.
